# Per-agent secrets — TEMPLATE
#
# Copy this directory to agents/<agent-name>/, then:
#
#   cp agents/<agent-name>/.env.example agents/<agent-name>/.env
#
# The .env is gitignored (the root .gitignore's ".env" pattern matches at any
# depth) and mounted read-only at /opt/data/profiles/<agent-name>/.env.
# Create it BEFORE "docker compose up -d" — the mount is written with
# create_host_path: false so a missing file stops the stack loudly instead of
# Docker silently creating a root-owned directory in its place.
#
# Only put a secret here when THIS agent must hold a different one from every
# other agent. Container-wide secrets belong in the root .env and reach every
# profile through the compose "environment:" list.
#
# Per-agent credentials are the access boundary (plan §0): scope each agent's
# integration in the upstream system to exactly what that agent may read.
# Prompting cannot enforce that; a scoped token can.

# Both names, one secret: the `notion` skill declares NOTION_API_KEY, the `ntn`
# CLI it drives reads NOTION_API_TOKEN. python-dotenv interpolates ${VAR}, so
# the key is rotated in one place. See agents/backoffice/.env.example.
#NOTION_API_KEY=ntn_...
#NOTION_API_TOKEN=${NOTION_API_KEY}
#OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...

# One Slack app per agent, so the tokens are per-agent by definition — the
# compose "environment:" list is container-wide and would hand every other
# persona this bot's identity. See agents/backoffice/ for a worked example
# (slack-manifest.yaml carries the scopes, config.yaml the channel behaviour).
#SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
#SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-...
#SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=...
